{{-- App chrome. Layout group 'app'.

    A FRAGMENT, not a document -- same contract as auth.stx and marketing.stx. stx
    synthesises the doctype, the html tag, the head and the body from config/ui.ts
    app.head merged with each page's head. Writing a doctype or an html tag here, even
    inside a comment (the test is an unanchored regex over the rendered string), makes
    hasDocumentShell() true and every page's title and description is dropped.

    Replaces the hand-written `<meta name="stx-layout" content="app">` that dashboard.stx
    and account.stx each carried. That meta is the OUTPUT of layout resolution, not an
    input to it: stx emits it from the `<!-- stx-layout: ... -->` marker only after a
    layout actually resolves, and deriveLayoutGroup reads the group from the layout's
    FILENAME. Asserting the meta by hand produced the right header while the mechanism
    behind it never ran, so the pages shared no chrome, no styles and no stacks -- and
    nothing would have reported a disagreement between the claim and the file.

    <main> lives HERE and pages must not declare their own: fragment extraction slices
    from the first <main> to the LAST </main>, so a second one makes the router swap the
    wrong span. It is deliberately unstyled, because the two pages disagree about their
    wrapper -- the dashboard wants full-bleed padding, account wants a centred max-w-3xl
    column -- so each yields its own <div> with the classes it already had. Same
    elements, same classes, one tag name different.

    There is no <style> block here any more, and there must not be one: a style block in
    a .stx file is what stx-standards rule 3 / §11.1 forbids, and the tokens it held are
    what §11.2 says belong in config/crosswind.ts exactly once. The :root palette, the
    body rule, .wordmark and ThemeToggle's ten rules are `preflights` entries there now,
    keyed off the `<meta name="stx-layout-group" content="app">` that stx emits from THIS
    FILE'S NAME -- so renaming this file repaints these pages, and the config says so.

    Before that, the palette was duplicated in dashboard.stx and account.stx byte for
    byte apart from --bar (dashboard only) and a longer --mono fallback chain, then
    deduped into one copy here. Both supersets survived the move to the config, so
    account still gains an unused variable and a better monospace stack.

    Everything page-specific stays in the page, pushed to @stack('styles') rather than
    left loose: once a layout applies, stx sweeps every orphan <style> out of the page
    and appends it AFTER the content, in the body.

    That stack sits INSIDE <main> below, which it did not until 2026-10-13. In
    @head it was outside the router's container and so was never removed, and
    whichever of these three pages the visitor entered on kept its rules live
    for the whole session -- /dashboard's 23k of CSS, including a `.panel` and a
    `.btn` that eight and six other files use in markup. --}}

<main>
{{-- Inside the container, not in @head. See the note in marketing.stx: a pushed
    <style> in the head outlives the page that pushed it, so entering on
    /dashboard applied its 23k of CSS to every route for the session. --}}
@stack('styles')
@yield('content')
</main>

@stack('scripts')
